// Access-path lowering rewrites a single-statement deep read (`out = a.b.c.d`) into a
// chain of single-field hops through fresh temporaries. Each temporary holds a *suffix*
// of the original path (e.g. `.c.d` after `.b` is stripped), and the cap algorithm only
// reconstructs the deep *prefix* paths -- so without closing the `paths` whitelist under
// suffixes the flow is dropped at the first hop. This is a regression test for that
// suffix closure: a depth-4 deep read must still carry taint end to end. The errsink
// check confirms field sensitivity survives lowering: a sibling field stays untainted.
def DeepRead() {
start:
  s = source(Secret);
  a.b.c.d = s;
  out = a.b.c.d;
  sink(out, Secret);

  a.b.c.e = "safe";
  other = a.b.c.e;
  errsink(other, Secret);
  return;
}
